class_name JsMath
extends RefCounted
## JavaScript-compatible numerics for the simulation (design section 5.2).
##
## The original game runs on V8 and the port has to reproduce its numbers bit for bit, because the random-number
## draws and every spawn depend on them. Godot's built-in functions are not bit-identical to V8 (measured on 200 000
## or more inputs per function against Chromium 141 / V8 14.1.146.11; the golden vectors of res://tests/golden/math.json
## are generated by tools/golden/math.mjs and checked against Chromium by tools/verify_golden_chromium.mjs), and they differ
## between platforms (glibc on desktop, Bionic on Android). So:
##
##   * ported from V8, bit-exact on every platform because only IEEE-754 additions, multiplications, divisions and
##     comparisons are used (Godot's Android and Linux builds use -ffp-contract=off, and every GDScript operation is a
##     separate step, so nothing is fused into an FMA):
##       sin, cos      glibc s_sin.c / branred.c as in V8's third_party/glibc (what Chromium's Math.sin / Math.cos call)
##       atan, atan2   fdlibm s_atan.c / e_atan2.c as in V8 src/base/ieee754.cc
##       exp, log      fdlibm e_exp.c / e_log.c as in V8 src/base/ieee754.cc
##       hypot         V8 Torque FastMathHypot (two arguments)
##       js_round      V8 CodeStubAssembler Float64Round (ceil based; keeps -0)
##       js_max, js_min, max3, min3, max5, min5, max_n, min_n, clamp   Float64Max / Float64Min (NaN wins, -0 < +0)
##       to_int32, to_uint32, i32, imul, shl, sar, shr_u   32-bit integer semantics on 64-bit ints
##   * IEEE-754 exact, so the engine's own functions are used: sqrt, floor, ceil, abs (thin wrappers), fmod (js_mod).
##   * pow is NOT bit-guaranteed: it handles the JavaScript special cases and then calls the platform pow. It is not
##     used by the simulation of milestone 1.
##
## Rules for simulation code: call these functions with the class name (JsMath.sin(x)). Inside this class an unqualified
## sin(x) is the ENGINE's function (utility functions win over members of the same name): the wrappers sqrt() and pow()
## below rely on that, and every internal call that means this class's own function is qualified (JsMath.atan). Never use
## in the simulation: Vector2 math (float32), round()/roundf(), maxf/minf/max/min/clamp
## builtins, fposmod, int() on floats that may be out of range or NaN, lerp, a float % float (compile error), and
## float literals with more than 15 significant digits (the engine's float parser is not correctly rounded). Constants
## below are written as `integer / 2^k`, which is exact, and checked against their bit patterns by self_check().
##
## Not re-entrant across threads: the slow paths use one shared 8-byte buffer and one result pair.
##
## Provenance of every constant: the comment on its line names the upstream file and symbol. tools/check_js_compat_consts.mjs
## (part of tools/check_generated.sh) compares every constant, its CONST_BITS entry and the two big tables with the upstream
## literals, so a mistake made in both the expression and CONST_BITS is caught too; self_check() (test_js_math.gd) checks
## that the engine evaluates the expressions to those bit patterns.
##
## Cost (x86-64 development VM, Godot 4.7.2 headless; the BENCH lines of test_js_math.gd): sin / cos about 0.8-1.0 us, atan2
## 0.7 us, exp 0.5 us, hypot 0.3 us per call, against 35-55 ns for the engine builtins. A scratch benchmark replaying the
## trig / exp / hypot calls of the original's per-entity code measured about 1.1 ms per step for 100 enemies (moveBasicEnemy +
## steerEnemy + smoothEnemyVelocity), 3.9 ms for 520 homing projectiles and 1.2 ms for 520 orbitals, against 0.2 / 0.6 / 0.3 ms
## with the builtins. The design 5.5 budget (Sim.step median <= 4 ms for 100 enemies and 520 projectiles) is therefore at risk
## once the kernels of S5/S6 exist: measure that scenario as soon as they do, and keep the bit-exact cost cuts in mind for
## them: compute 1 - exp(-k * dt) once per constant k when dt is FIXED_DT (same inputs, same bits), do not recompute the
## sin / cos / atan2 of an angle that is already known, and consider a fused sin+cos entry point or (design 12) JsMath in a
## GDExtension built with -ffp-contract=off (the same IEEE operations; an estimate of 20-40 times faster, not measured) if the budget is still missed.

